###############################################################################
# Items API — the happy path
#
# One file, either stack. Both implement the same frozen contract, so switching
# backend is switching the variable below and nothing else — which is the claim
# this file exists to let you check by hand.
#
# Runs in VS Code (REST Client extension) or any JetBrains IDE. Send a request
# with the "Send Request" link above it.
#
# Start a backend first:
#   api-node    npm run start:dev     ->  :3000
#   api-python  python manage.py runserver  ->  :8000
###############################################################################

@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000
# @baseUrl = http://localhost:8000

### List every item
# 200 with a bare JSON array — no envelope, no pagination. Ordered by id ascending,
# so the response is identical across runs and across both stacks.
# An empty collection is 200 with [], never 404.
GET {{baseUrl}}/items
Accept: application/json


### Fetch one item
# Change the id if your database has different rows.
GET {{baseUrl}}/items/1
Accept: application/json


### Create an item
# 201, a Location header of /items/{id}, and the full created item including the
# server-assigned id and createdAt.
#
# Two things to look at in the response body:
#   - `price` is a JSON number, not a string. Neither stack does this by default —
#     Prisma returns a Decimal object and DRF stringifies decimals — so both were
#     told to explicitly.
#   - `createdAt` is a UTC instant ending in Z. Node emits milliseconds, Django
#     emits microseconds when present. Both are valid ISO-8601; the contract does
#     not pin the fractional-second precision further because it would buy nothing.
#
# Re-sending this exact request answers 409: the sku is already taken.
POST {{baseUrl}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Standing Desk",
  "sku": "DESK-STD-1",
  "quantity": 4,
  "price": 649.00
}


### Create an item — surrounding whitespace in `name` is trimmed
# The contract trims before validating, so the stored name has no padding. Worth
# sending once: it is the difference between validating one value and storing another.
POST {{baseUrl}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "   Laptop Stand   ",
  "sku": "STAND-AL-2",
  "quantity": 9,
  "price": 79.95
}


### Create an item — quantity 0 is valid
# The contract's minimum is 0, not 1. Out of stock is a real state, and 0 is the
# value most likely to be mishandled by a falsy check somewhere.
POST {{baseUrl}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Cable Organiser",
  "sku": "CBL-ORG-3",
  "quantity": 0,
  "price": 12.50
}


### Create an item — unknown fields are ignored, not rejected
# `id` and `hacked` are discarded and the server assigns its own id. This is DRF's
# default behaviour and is reachable in Nest with ValidationPipe({ whitelist: true }),
# so both stacks land here without fighting their framework.
#
# Check the response: the id is not 99.
POST {{baseUrl}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Monitor Arm",
  "sku": "ARM-DUAL-4",
  "quantity": 6,
  "price": 129.99,
  "id": 99,
  "hacked": true
}
